+# Hay bales and breakable wooden crates
+
+Owner request, 2026-10-12. BP01: hay bales lose shape and shed harmless hay. BP02: their remaining core becomes smaller and lighter. BP03: wooden crates move under light contact and accumulate damage under heavier crashes. BP04: broken crates leave movable wood pieces with small driving effects. BP05: exact replay/rewind, per-attempt reset, five ordinary laps, local storage and pinned contracts. BP06: original shared models/icons, actual captures and bounded cost. Preserve G01/R01/T01-T04/V04-V06/Q01.
+
+## Behaviour
+
+Choose **Objects → Props → Hay bale** or **Wooden crate**. The existing `crate` identifier is retained, so saved crates receive the new behaviour. Both support the established place/move/rotate/size/duplicate/delete/undo/save flow and are available in all five biomes. Neither is baked into an impassable native wall. No transient destruction state is saved into a track document.
+
+A hay bale starts as a tied 2 × 1.2 m bundle. Contacts at at least 1 m/s shed material and expose progressively frayed, uneven shapes. The remaining fraction controls actual mass (85 kg at standard size, proportional to remaining volume) and the core's linear scale/radius (cube root of the remaining fraction). Object size scales mass by volume. A worn bale is easier to push and causes less car speed loss. After the final small remainder disperses, the core has no collision. Loose hay has **zero mass and zero car, collision, grip or tyre-contamination effect**; its short outward movement and eventual ground scatter are presentation carried in the deterministic state.
+
+A standard wooden crate has a 22 kg effective mass. Gentle contacts move/turn it without reducing integrity. Relative impacts above 4 m/s accumulate damage; repeated heavier crashes open/split boards, then remove the crate core and release six planks. Distinct impacts are latched until separation: sustained contact or following an already-moving crate at the same speed does not count as a new crash every tick. Reverse contacts push in the actual direction of travel. Wood pieces slide, rotate and settle, remain across laps, and can be pushed again. Their oriented planar contact produces a small momentum-based speed loss (at most 0.035 m/s per piece/contact tick) and slight off-centre yaw response. Wood does not become an oil-like grip patch or an impassable wall. Ground objects do not contact an airborne or elevated car.
+
+Damage and scatter persist across laps. New attempts reset them completely, including immediate intact visual restoration during the countdown before the first driving tick. Rewind restores integrity, impact latches, core positions/velocities, individual fragments and their motion; resumed physics reproduces the exact later branch. Existing collision observer output supplies crash feedback without introducing an external audio dependency. Mass/damage thresholds and the small wood response remain owner-review balance values.
+
+## Implementation and contracts
+
+`BreakableProps` extends the existing canonical light-prop path using only ordered numeric arrays and the fixed 120 Hz step. Other light props retain their exact previous path. There is no 3D rigid-body solver, runtime random generator, wall clock, network or file operation in the simulation. Up to 96 authored crates can produce 576 physical planks. Cosmetic loose hay is capped at 128 clumps; replacing old hay never discards physical wood. The conservative combined stress bound is 704 fragments. Moving pieces check native solid scenery; settled pieces avoid world queries. Fragment-to-fragment and crate-to-crate rigid-body collisions, punctures and structural vehicle damage are outside this slice.
+
+Three original prepared damage meshes per kind are shared across instances, with one shared matte sRGB material. Loose hay and wood use two MultiMesh batches with original fibre/plank geometry. The existing one-shot palette renderer uses the same intact models. Rendering changes visibility, scale, transforms and buffers only; it does not mutate canonical state. Prepared scenery clones preserve object bindings and isolate mutable MultiMesh buffers.
+
+Physics was advanced from `arcade-planar-0.54` to the breakable slice's `0.55`; the shared tree subsequently advanced to `0.56`, which is preserved. Compiler is `creator-compiler-26`. Creator documents remain `creator-track-v5`, adding `hay_bale` to the allowed object enum. Recorder schema 12 preserves snapshot fields 0–22 and appends `[prop_destruction, prop_fragments]` at field 23 (24 total fields). Input replay remains `bmt-replay-v1`, ordinary races remain five laps, and rules remain `standing-5-road-gates-v20`. Historical records, completed-ticket results and recorded inputs are retained; new PB/track contracts have their new pinned keys. Old documents/files were not deleted.
+
+Before updating golden identities, the contract audit checked all eight exact compiler-25 raster fingerprints and identical prepared geometry after normalising the version, 1,800 full no-breakable physics snapshots in all five biomes, and 400 exact legacy cone/board/barrel states. The new starter hash is `d73eb5e758e96a7333e3da9c2a4ad238b3d57718ad9acb62867d7965898c7dd4`. The local backend test fixture updates only its measured manifest pins; its document, inputs, timing and vehicle configuration hashes remain unchanged. Standalone verifier staging includes the new canonical script and TrackTheme's required winter-sky preload. Race acceptance remains trajectory-only; full replay is used only by the explicitly invoked offline diagnostics/tests.
+
+Changed runtime files: `game/vehicle/breakable_props.gd` (+UID), `light_props.gd`, `vehicle_state.gd`, the physics version in `vehicle_simulation.gd`, `game/persistence/handling_recorder.gd`, `game/editor/creator_catalog.gd`, `creator_objects.gd`, the compiler version in `track_compiler.gd`, `game/track/breakable_prop_visual.gd` (+UID), `prop_equipment.gd`, the prop-cache version in `creator_prop_visual.gd`, and placed-object/presentation sections of `native_track_visual.gd`. Contract/test files: creator schema, raster fixture, `test_creator.gd`, `test_tracks.gd`, `test_kerbs.gd`, `test_winter_compiler_contract.gd`, new `test_breakable_props.gd`, `test_breakable_contract.gd`, `capture_breakable_props.gd` (+UIDs), `scripts/dev.ps1` validation wiring, `backend/deploy/stage_verifier.py`, `backend/tests/test_verifier.py`, and its measured `verified_oval.json` manifest. Documentation: this file, AGENTS/status/phase entries and evidence.
+
+## Actual verification
+
+Commands use pinned `4.7.2.stable.official.ed1daf0bf` through `powershell -NoProfile -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scripts/dev.ps1`. Tests use isolated local records; no live account or backend write is involved.
+
+| Arguments / command | Actual outcome |
+| --- | --- |
+| `-Action TestScript -ScriptPath res://tests/test_breakable_props.gd` and Mobile with `-Graphical -RenderingMethod mobile -RenderingDriver vulkan` | Final 46 checks: progressive mass/size, harmless hay, gentle/heavy/reverse crate contact, small dissipative wood response, bridge/flight isolation, five actual driven input replays, exact restored branch, immediate visual reset and conservative maximum debris bounds. |
+| `-Action TestScript -ScriptPath res://tests/test_breakable_contract.gd -TestArgument '--refresh-breakable-golden'` | 2,219 checks, zero failures before writing new raster identities; final non-writing repeat also 2,219/0. |
+| `-Action TestScript -ScriptPath res://tests/capture_breakable_props.gd -Graphical` and Mobile equivalent | 22 checks per renderer, zero failures: actual canonically generated intact/deformed/broken/scattered states, close-ups, prepared-cache rewind restoration, 1280/960 editor palette and real-file save/reload. |
+| `-Action TestObjects`, `TestHazards`, `TestKerbs`, `TestSuspension`, `TestRewind` | Respectively 71, 160, 31, 59 and 61 checks, zero failures. |
+| `-Action TestDriving`, `TestHandling`, `TestTracks`, `TestCreator` | 72, 88, 167 and 113 checks, zero failures, including complete historical diagnostic races and exact JSON replay at multiple render batch sizes. |
+| `-Action TestScript -ScriptPath res://tests/test_speed_bumps.gd` | 9,045 checks, zero failures; existing timed suspension advantage, energy bounds and all-car replay retained. |
+| `-Action TestScript -ScriptPath res://tests/test_prop_refinement.gd` | 594 checks, zero failures across all five biomes and the additional hay model/prepared damage geometry. |
+| `-Action TestApp` | Final corrected smoke run: 99 checks, zero failures. |
+| `-Action Doctor` | Exact engine/templates found; DN2103 attached at the final check. |
+| `-Action TestScript -ScriptPath res://tests/test_script_parse.gd` | Actual complete parse: 596 scripts, zero failures. |
+| `.tools/backend-venv/Scripts/python.exe build/breakable_props/refresh_backend_fixture.py` | Actual canonical manifests measured; recorded document/input/timing fields preserved. |
+| `.tools/backend-venv/Scripts/python.exe -m pytest backend/tests/test_verifier.py -q` | 69 passed, including historical physics-0.50 exact rejected-input replay and standalone canonical packaging. Existing Starlette/httpx deprecation warning remains; no dependency installed. |
+
+The 704-fragment synthetic upper-bound test took 176.827 ms for 240 canonical steps on the desktop Mobile run (about 0.737 ms/step), and an earlier headless run took 205.303 ms. This isolates the prop routine; it is not a whole-game/phone frame budget or thermal measurement. Shared meshes avoid creating separate damage geometry for every placed object. Physical-phone cost and dense authored layouts remain unmeasured.
+
+Captures: [editor palette](evidence/breakable-props-2026-10-12/gl_compatibility/editor-palette-960.png), [deformed/cracked objects](evidence/breakable-props-2026-10-12/gl_compatibility/deformed-and-cracked.png), [worn bale](evidence/breakable-props-2026-10-12/gl_compatibility/worn-bale-detail.png), [wood pieces](evidence/breakable-props-2026-10-12/gl_compatibility/wood-debris-detail.png), [fully scattered material](evidence/breakable-props-2026-10-12/mobile/loose-hay-and-wood.png), [rewind restored](evidence/breakable-props-2026-10-12/mobile/rewind-restored.png). Stage photos show contacts and settling generated by canonical stepping with diagnostic positioning between distinct impacts; they do not claim a continuous race recording. Five actual driving approaches are separately tested for exact input replay. Before sources, old fixtures, actual logs, final test reports, scoped patch and hash ledger are retained in `docs/evidence/breakable-props-2026-10-12`; the isolated two-commit Git checkpoint is `build/breakable_props/checkpoint-repo`. Main branch/index and unrelated concurrent edits are preserved.
+
+Initial failures are retained: fixture callable/catalog API declarations and optional three-field input decoding were corrected; repeated-impact tests now wait for outgoing objects to settle rather than misclassifying equal-speed following as another crash. A negative diagnostic rotation was replaced by its valid 345° equivalent, and capture framing now includes projected debris bounds. Standalone verifier failures exposed omitted canonical/sky dependencies, which are now packaged without weakening failure checks; all 69 verifier tests then passed. A full editor import encountered the concurrently locked Windows audio extension. A later app smoke attempt encountered an in-progress surface-shader null-flag error; its corrected rerun passed all 99 app checks. No failure was made green by removing an invariant.
+
+The final Doctor check detected a DN2103 Android phone. No physical-device play/performance test was performed for this task. No Android export/install, production deployment, live data migration or physics merge was performed for this task. Matching backend/compiler/catalog rollout remains necessary before this new client contract can be used for live competitive starts or track publication; deployment remains separately authorised. Owner acceptance remains for damage thresholds, movement/wood feel, material density/appearance and icon recognition; device acceptance remains for touch placement, bright-light readability, sustained frame pacing, memory and thermals. Restart the client to load the new kit.
